## Who owns `install_script` and `uninstall_script`

A Fleet Maintained App's install and uninstall scripts are maintained upstream:
Fleet generates them and keeps them current as it publishes new versions of the
app. Ownership of those two attributes therefore follows the configuration.

**Omitted from the configuration — Fleet owns the script.** Terraform stores no
copy of it, reports no drift when Fleet changes it, and never sends the field
when updating the title for some other reason. Upstream script updates reach
your hosts on their own.

Be deliberate about this: Terraform is not a control over a script it does not
own. A change made through the Fleet UI or API is neither reported by
`terraform plan` nor reverted by `terraform apply`. Fleet's own activity log is
the audit trail for those changes.

**Declared in the configuration — Terraform owns the script.** The configured
value is authoritative: it is applied on create, an edit made through the Fleet
UI is reported as drift and reverted on the next apply, and Fleet's upstream
updates to that script no longer apply.

Install scripts run with administrative privilege on every targeted host, so
declaring them is the safer default for titles that matter to your security
posture — an agent, an EDR sensor, anything privileged. Declaring is what makes
an unauthorized change both visible and self-correcting. The trade is that you
take on keeping the script working across new versions of the app, which is
exactly what Fleet does for you otherwise.

**Removing the attribute hands ownership back to Fleet** without clearing the
script that is in place. The update omits the field rather than sending an empty
one, so the last-applied script keeps working until Fleet next regenerates it.

Setting an attribute to `""` is not a way to hand a script back — remove the
attribute instead. Fleet rejects an empty script for package types that require
one (for example a `.zip` installer answers `Install script is required for .zip
packages`).

After `terraform import` both attributes start null, so the imported title's
scripts stay with Fleet until you declare them.

### Upgrading from an earlier provider version

These two attributes used to be `Computed`, so Fleet's script was mirrored into
state even when the configuration did not mention it. If you have Fleet
Maintained Apps in state from an earlier version, the first plan after upgrading
shows a one-time `install_script`/`uninstall_script` change to `null` for every
title whose configuration omits them. That diff only drops Terraform's copy of
the value: applying it sends no script to Fleet and leaves the script on the
title untouched. Titles that declare the attributes are unaffected.

One consequence to check before upgrading: a reference such as
`fleetdm_software_fleet_maintained_app.example.install_script` now resolves to
`null` while the attribute is undeclared. Read the script from the catalog
instead — `data.fleetdm_fleet_maintained_app.example.install_script`.

